[" Here's a C function named `scan` that follows your specifications:

```c
#include <stdio.h>
#include <string.h>

// Function prototype
size_t scan(char* buffer, size_t size);

// Function definition
size_t scan(char* buffer, size_t size) {
    // Ensure the buffer and size are valid
    if (!buffer || !size) return 0;

    // Initialize variables for reading characters
    int c, count = 0;

    // Use a getline-like loop to read the input
    while ((c = getchar()) != '\n') {
        // Check if we've reached the buffer limit before writing to it
        if (count < size - 1)
            buffer[count++] = c;

        // If we reach the buffer limit, break the loop early
        if (count == size - 1)
            break;
    }

    // Store the number of characters read in the buffer
    buffer[count] = '\0';

    // Return the count excluding the newline character
    return count;
}
```

This `scan` function behaves similarly to the built-in `fgets` function, but it doesn't include the newline character in the buffer and allows you to read more than one line at a time if needed. The function takes a pointer to a buffer and the size of the buffer as arguments, ensuring that it won't overflow during the operation."]

